'''Coruxón''' ({{IPA-bl|kˌoɾuksˈon}}; {{Literal translation|owls}}; {{Langx|kp|îakurutu kûara|4=place of the owls}}) is a [[Cantons of Balisca|borough]] of the [[Metropolitan City of Castejón]], within the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]]. Located in the relatively flat western portion of the city, it is the fourth largest borough of Castejón by area. It is bordered by [[Llanito]] to the south, [[Charxes]] to the west and [[Alagón river]] to the north and west.

It was reported to have a population of 3,453,723 in the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], making it the second-most populous borough in the Castejón Metropolis and second-most populous [[Cantons of Balisca|third-level subdivision]] in the Baliscan Confederation (behind [[Llanito]]). Were it an independent city, Coruxón would be the sixth most-populous in Balisca. Approximately 13.3 percent of the residents of the borough are foreign-born.

Coruxón was established in 1545 as one of the [[Original Settlements of the Alpujarra|original settlements]] of the [[Alpujarra]]. The city developed in the post-independence period as a market and trading city due to its advantageous location on the Alagón. It remained an independent incorporated city (and previously an authorized village and town within the provisions of the [[Charter of Metropolitan Castejón]]) until October 1902, when, after a long political campaign and public relations battle during the 1890s, according to the new Metropolitan Charter of "Greater Castejón," [[consolidation of Castejón|Coruxón was consolidated]] with other communes to form the modern ''Castejón Metropolis,'' surrounding [[Salto Bay]] with six constituent boroughs. The borough continues, however, to maintain a [[Culture of Roma|distinct culture]].

